diff --git a/docs/decisions/0009-due-times.md b/docs/decisions/0009-due-times.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1754903
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/0009-due-times.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# 9. Due times
+
+Date: 2025-11-15
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The help desk is starting to promise response times: 4 hours for urgent tickets, 24 for high, 72 for normal and 168
+for low. Agents and the support leads need to see when each ticket is due and which open ones are late. The promise is
+made when a ticket is created, so it must not move when the ticket's priority is changed later, or when the help desk
+changes the hours.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will give every ticket a `due` time: its creation time plus the response time of its priority.
+
+- The due time is worked out once, by the store when it opens the ticket, and stored on the ticket. Nothing recomputes
+  it: not a change of priority, not a change of the response times.
+- The response times are settings (`DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL`, `DUE_HOURS_LOW`), so the help
+  desk changes them without a code change. A change applies to tickets created after the restart.
+- `GET /tickets?overdue=true` lists the tickets that are open or in progress and whose due time has passed. It narrows
+  the other filters; it does not add to them.
+- The data file keeps `due` as an optional field of format version 1. Tickets of a file written before this release have
+  none: the store gives each its due time when it loads the file, from the ticket's own creation time and priority, and
+  the service saves at once, so from then on the file holds it. We do not move to version 2, because release 1.4.0
+  refuses any version but 1 and ignores fields it does not know: with version 1, going back to 1.4.0 starts on the
+  file this release wrote and shows every ticket.
+- The store keeps and saves `due` whatever the settings, but shows it, and answers `overdue`, only when `DUE_TIMES=on`.
+  Until then the API answers as before. This lets an operator deploy first, check that the data file holds every due
+  time, and then switch the feature on.
+- The audit log does not record due times: setting one is not a change that anyone made.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A rollback to 1.4.0 loses the `due` fields at its next save (it writes only the fields it knows). The new release
+  works them out again at its next start, from the creation time and the priority the ticket has then.
+- A ticket created at one priority and raised later is still due by the response time of its original priority.
+- The listing reads each ticket's due time: it is a filter over all tickets, which is fine at our size.
